Organizing and maintaining a font directory is probably one ofthe most difficult aspects of managing a TeX system.  Thereare many ways to organize this directory, but the methodI recommend is as follows:  1) Keep all fonts in "pk" format.  After using MetaFont tocreate a new font, immediately use "gftopk" to convert itinto PK format and delete the GF format file.  This cansave you as much as 50% on disk space.  MetaFont alsobuilds a ".log" file, which can be deleted if nothingwent wrong, and a ".tfm" file.  Probably, the TFM fileduplicates a file in FONTS/TFM, in which case you canjust delete it.  If you don't already have a TFM fileof the same name, copy the new TFM file into FONTS/TFM.  2) Most printing devices have "square aspect ratio," meaningthat their vertical and horizontal resolutions are the same.I suggest sorting all such fonts by their resolution, whichis the number in the file extension.  For example, store"cmr10.347pk" in the directory "FONTS/PK347".  This helps keepthe directories from becoming too large.  I've built thisscheme into the port of the Beebe DVI drivers to OS9.  3) Some printers (notably 9-pin Epson compatibles, whichprint at 240dpi horizontally and 216dpi vertically) do nothave square aspect ratio.  You should keep these separate fromfonts that do have a square aspect ratio, in order to avoidconfusion.  The DVIEPS driver I ported to OS9 automaticallylooks in "FONTS/EPSON" for all of it's font files.  (Italso checks the "PK###" directories, but those fonts will comeout about 15% too tall when printed on the Epson, so don'tcount on them for the best output.)To summarize:   FONTS/TFM - Keep all ".tfm" files here.  Add new ones as               you find or create them.   FONTS/PK### - Store "square aspect ratio" fonts in these               directories, sorted by resolution.  You'll end               up with a large number of directories, but it's               better than having all those fonts in one directory.   FONTS/EPSON - Store Epson 9-pin 240x216dpi fonts here.   FONTS/<device> - Store fonts for other non-square printers               in an appropriate directory for that device.   FONTS/VF - Store "virtual fonts" here.  You probably don't have               (or know how to use) virtual fonts, but if you do,               here's where to keep them.